-- Relational Algebra Example: Employee and Department Relations

-- Suppose we have two relations (tables):

-- Employee(EmpID, EmpName, DeptID, Salary)
-- Department(DeptID, DeptName, Location)

-- Sample Data:
-- Employee:
-- | EmpID | EmpName  | DeptID | Salary |
-- |-------|----------|--------|--------|
-- | 1     | Alice    | 10     | 70000  |
-- | 2     | Bob      | 20     | 60000  |
-- | 3     | Charlie  | 10     | 65000  |
-- | 4     | David    | 30     | 55000  |

-- Department:
-- | DeptID | DeptName    | Location  |
-- |--------|-------------|-----------|
-- | 10     | HR          | New York  |
-- | 20     | Engineering | San Jose  |
-- | 30     | Marketing   | Chicago   |

-- 1. Selection (σ): Find employees with salary > 60000

σ_Salary > 60000(Employee)

-- Result:
-- | EmpID | EmpName | DeptID | Salary |
-- |-------|---------|--------|--------|
-- | 1     | Alice   | 10     | 70000  |
-- | 3     | Charlie | 10     | 65000  |

-- 2. Projection (π): Get only EmpName and Salary from Employee

π_EmpName, Salary(Employee)

-- Result:
-- | EmpName | Salary |
-- |---------|--------|
-- | Alice   | 70000  |
-- | Bob     | 60000  |
-- | Charlie | 65000  |
-- | David   | 55000  |

-- 3. Join (⨝): Find employee names along with their department names

Employee ⨝ Employee.DeptID = Department.DeptID Department

-- Result:
-- | EmpID | EmpName | DeptID | Salary | DeptID | DeptName    | Location  |
-- |-------|---------|--------|--------|--------|-------------|-----------|
-- | 1     | Alice   | 10     | 70000  | 10     | HR          | New York  |
-- | 3     | Charlie | 10     | 65000  | 10     | HR          | New York  |
-- | 2     | Bob     | 20     | 60000  | 20     | Engineering | San Jose  |
-- | 4     | David   | 30     | 55000  | 30     | Marketing   | Chicago   |

-- 4. Rename (ρ): Rename DeptName to DepartmentName in Department relation

ρ_DepartmentName/DeptName(Department)

-- Result:
-- | DeptID | DepartmentName | Location  |
-- |--------|----------------|-----------|
-- | 10     | HR             | New York  |
-- | 20     | Engineering    | San Jose  |
-- | 30     | Marketing      | Chicago   |

-- 5. Set Difference (-): Find employees who are NOT in Department 10 (HR)

σ_DeptID ≠ 10(Employee)

-- Result:
-- | EmpID | EmpName | DeptID | Salary |
-- |-------|---------|--------|--------|
-- | 2     | Bob     | 20     | 60000  |
-- | 4     | David   | 30     | 55000  |

-- 6. Union (∪): Suppose we have two sets of employees from two different queries,
-- find all employees who have salary > 60000 or belong to DeptID 30

σ_Salary > 60000(Employee) ∪ σ_DeptID = 30(Employee)

-- Result:
-- | EmpID | EmpName | DeptID | Salary |
-- |-------|---------|--------|--------|
-- | 1     | Alice   | 10     | 70000  |
-- | 3     | Charlie | 10     | 65000  |
-- | 4     | David   | 30     | 55000  |

-- 7. Intersection (∩): Find employees who have salary > 60000 AND belong to DeptID 10

σ_Salary > 60000(Employee) ∩ σ_DeptID = 10(Employee)

-- Result:
-- | EmpID | EmpName | DeptID | Salary |
-- |-------|---------|--------|--------|
-- | 1     | Alice   | 10     | 70000  |
-- | 3     | Charlie | 10     | 65000  |

-- Summary:
-- Relational Algebra operators used:
-- σ (Selection), π (Projection), ⨝ (Join), ρ (Rename), ∪ (Union), - (Difference), ∩ (Intersection)
Selection (σ) filters rows based on a condition.
Projection (π) selects specific columns.
Join (⨝) combines rows from two relations based on a common attribute.
Rename (ρ) changes attribute names for clarity.
Union (∪) combines tuples from two relations (duplicates removed).
Difference (-) finds tuples in one relation but not in another.
Intersection (∩) finds tuples common to both relations.
This example demonstrates basic relational algebra operations on simple employee and department relations, which are foundational for querying relational databases.
